有些不知生存的意义，于是我继续学习拉丁语。

# Story 3 The Adventures of Io

## 涉及语法

>First & Second Conjugations: Future & Imperfect;
Adjectives in -er(第五课)

### 将来时与未完成时 (Futūrum et Imperfectum)

同样是先处理第一第二变位法的词汇。这部分动词通过直接在现在时词干与人称词尾之间插入将来时与未完成时的标志来表示时态变化。未完成时近乎等效于英语的过去进行时。现在时、将来时与未完成时构成了*现在时系统*。（未来还会有*完成时系统*与*完成被动系统*）

将来时标志 *-bi-* 未完成时标志 *-ba-*

范例动词： amō, amāre, amāvī, amātum (爱); monēo, monēre, monuī, monitum (提醒)

||单数 (Singulāris)|复数 (Plūrālis)|
|:---|:---|:---|
|**第一人称 (1st Pers.)**|amābam (我曾在爱)|amābāmus (我们曾在爱)|
|**第二人称 (2nd Pers.)**|amābās (你曾在爱)|amābātis (你们曾在爱)|
|**第三人称 (3rd Pers.)**|amābat (他/她/它曾在爱)|amābant (他们曾在爱)|

||单数 (Singulāris)|复数 (Plūrālis)|
|:---|:---|:---|
|**第一人称 (1st Pers.)**|amābō (我将爱)|amābimus (我们将爱)|
|**第二人称 (2nd Pers.)**|amābis (你将爱)|amābitis (你们将爱)|
|**第三人称 (3rd Pers.)**|amābit (他/她/它将爱)|amābunt (他们将爱)|

||单数 (Singulāris)|复数 (Plūrālis)|
|:---|:---|:---|
|**第一人称 (1st Pers.)**|monēbam (我曾在提醒)|monēbāmus (我们曾在提醒)|
|**第二人称 (2nd Pers.)**|monēbās (你曾在提醒)|monēbātis (你们曾在提醒)|
|**第三人称 (3rd Pers.)**|monēbat (他/她/它曾在提醒)|monēbant (他们曾在提醒)|

||单数 (Singulāris)|复数 (Plūrālis)|
|:---|:---|:---|
|**第一人称 (1st Pers.)**|monēbō (我将提醒)|monēbimus (我们将提醒)|
|**第二人称 (2nd Pers.)**|monēbis (你将提醒)|monēbitis (你们将提醒)|
|**第三人称 (3rd Pers.)**|monēbit (他/她/它将提醒)|monēbunt (他们将提醒)|

### 以 *-er* 结尾的形容词

这部分没什么好说的，就是词根要关注阴性和中性形式。

## 故事介绍

A myth that explains the reason behind a natural phenomenon, as the following story does, is called "aetiological."

## 正文

{{< book >}}

Iuppiter, rēx deōrum, pulchram Īō amābat, sed īram Iūnōnis metuēbat. Mūtāvit igitur fōrmam Īōnis: "Iūnō nōn fēminam, sed bovem vidēbit," Iuppiter cōgitābat. Iūnō nōn stulta erat: "Habēsne dōnum, mī vir? Dabisne bellam bovem Iūnōnī? Dā, sī mē amās!" Iuppiter igitur Iūnōnī bovem dedit. Cum bove remanēbat magnus custōs, Argus. Argus centum oculōs habēbat. Mercurius Argum superāvit, sed Īō nōndum lībera erat: malus asīlus cum bove manēbat. Īō errābat per terrās; multōs populōs vidēbat, sed vēram fōrmam suam nōn habēbat. Misera fēmina! Habēbisne semper fōrmam bovis? Nōnne satis est tua poena? 

Ira Iūnōnis nōn perpetua erat: Iuppiter Īōnī hūmānam fōrmam dedit; tum Īō fīlium genuit. Centum oculōs Argī vidēbitis in caudā pāvōnis.

<split>

Jupiter, the king of the gods, was loving beautiful Io, but was fearing the ire of Juno. So he changed the form of Io: " Juno will not see a woman, but a cow." Jupiter was thinking. Juno was not stupid: "Do you have a gift, my man? Will you give this beautiful cow to Juno? Give me, if you love me!" So Jupiter gave the cow to Juno. Argus, the huge watchman was staying with the cow. Argus was having one hundred eyes. Mecury conquered Argus, but Io was not yet free: a bad gadfly was staying with the cow. Io was wandering through the lands; she was seeing many people, but was not having her own true form. Poor woman! Will you alway have the form of a cow? Is your penalty not enough?

The ire of Juno was not forever: Jupiter gave Io the form of a human; then Io gave birth to a son. You will see the hundred eyes of Argus on the tail of peacock.

{{< /book >}}

## 评述

虽然是奥维德写的古罗马版本，但大家都知道这玩意就是古希腊经典款的宙斯赫拉与夹在中间的可怜男女小三系列故事。相关人士名单可太长了……光是我自己能记得的就有卡利斯托和塞墨勒，还有水瓶座的加尼米德。

我问Gemini如何理解宙斯的诸多外遇对象，Gemini师傅给出的我个人觉得最有意思的解释是，宙斯的每一次“出轨”，往往代表着一次历史上的“部落兼并”或“宗教融合”。这是记录在神话里的、崇拜宙斯的民族征服崇拜本地女神/仙女的区域的“史料”信息。

